  "solution": {
    "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><munder><mrow><mover><mrow><mover><mrow><munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">*</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi></mrow></mover></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mover><mo>—</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mtext>&nbsp;&nbsp;</mtext></mrow></munder><munder><mrow><mover><mrow><mover><mrow><munder><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">*</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi></mrow></mover></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mover><mo>—</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mtext>&nbsp;&nbsp;</mtext></mrow></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math><br /><br />It has two chiral centres so optical isomers <math><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math><br /><br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/25_solydche.JPG\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"width: 350px; height: 143px;\" /><br /><br />Structure (I) and (II) form a pair of enantiomers. Similarly, structure (III) and (IV) also form a pair entiomers. Structure (I) or (II) does not have a plane of symmetry bisecting the bond between the two asymmetric carbon atoms. Hence, this compound exists in four isomeric forms, which are optically active.",
